# FlushCrowd Environment Configuration Reference
#
# FlushCrowd does NOT use an automatic runtime dotenv loader (.env is not loaded at runtime).
# Do not copy this file expecting the app to automatically read values from .env.
# Configuration is handled through platform-specific files and compile-time Dart defines.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CONFIGURATION MATRIX
# ==============================================================================
#
# 1. Environment & App Check (compile-time --dart-define):
#    - APP_ENV: development | staging | production
#      Example: flutter run --dart-define=APP_ENV=staging
#    - ENABLE_APP_CHECK: true | false (defaults to true)
#      Example: flutter run --dart-define=ENABLE_APP_CHECK=false
#
# 2. Google Maps Platform Keys (platform-native configuration):
#    - Android: Placed in android/local.properties (gitignored) as:
#        MAPS_API_KEY=your_restricted_android_maps_api_key_here
#    - iOS: Placed in ios/Flutter/Secrets.xcconfig (gitignored) as:
#        GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your_restricted_ios_maps_api_key_here
#    (Both files are strictly gitignored. Never commit API keys.)
#
# 3. Firebase Configuration (platform-native configuration files):
#    - Android: Download google-services.json from Firebase Console and place at:
#        android/app/google-services.json
#    - iOS: Download GoogleService-Info.plist from Firebase Console and place at:
#        ios/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist
#    (Both files are strictly gitignored. Never commit Firebase config files.)
#
# 4. App Check Debug Token:
#    - When using Debug providers (development & staging), the Firebase SDK emits
#      a debug token to the console at startup.
#    - Register that token in Firebase Console -> App Check -> Apps -> Manage debug tokens.
#    - Tokens are managed through Firebase Console; never commit debug tokens.
